# Erdős Problem #99 — global pentagon two-window escape

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** active `research/c2-closure-sol-20260914-r5` branch
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Run type:** closing-proof reduction / exact face-degree and train-incidence composition
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Claim class:** proof-only consequence awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The existing exceptional-singleton two-window gate uses singleton trains to force a
population of pentagons and then invokes the odd-face parity lemma.  The singleton
localization is unnecessary for obtaining a global two-window escape.  The AG42 face
floor already supplies linearly many exceptional faces, while the degree-excess
currency `Q-f` pays every exceptional face that is not a pentagon.

Put

\[
X:=Q-f
\]

and, for projective directions `[u],[v]` and a fixed

\[
0<\epsilon<\epsilon_5:=2\arcsin(1/10),
\]

let `T_off(u,v;epsilon)` be the number of complete stopped trains whose projective
direction lies outside both `epsilon`-windows.

Then every canonical full-core Type-II survivor satisfies

\[
\boxed{
X+2T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)
\ge (f_*-o(1))D,
}
\tag{GP.1}
\]

for **every** pair `[u],[v]`, where

\[
f_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00021
=0.167124643466509\ldots,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{GP.2}
\]

Thus outer detour, singleton trains, and rhombic Mantel slack are not needed to force
three-directional complexity.  In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{{Q-f\over D},{T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\over D}\right\}
\ge {f_*\over3}-o(1)
=0.055708214488836\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{GP.3}
\]

If `Q-f=o(D)`, then for every fixed pair of projective directions,

\[
\boxed{
T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)
\ge\left({f_*\over2}-o(1)\right)D
=0.083562321733254\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{GP.4}
\]

## 1. Degree excess pays all nonpentagons

Let `n_d` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces of degree `d>=5`.
Then

\[
f=\sum_{d\ge5}n_d,
\qquad
Q=\sum_{d\ge5}(d-4)n_d,
\]

so

\[
X=Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d.
\tag{GP.5}
\]

Let

\[
p_5:=n_5,
\qquad
N_{\ge6}:=\sum_{d\ge6}n_d.
\]

Every summand in (GP.5) for `d>=6` is at least one. Therefore

\[
N_{\ge6}\le X
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{p_5=f-N_{\ge6}\ge f-X.}
\tag{GP.6}
\]

No bounded-degree cutoff is used.

## 2. Every pentagon forces an off-two-window terminal occurrence

The odd-face two-window theorem proves that every closed unit equilateral pentagon has,
for any prescribed `[u],[v]` and any fixed `epsilon<epsilon_5`, at least one boundary
edge whose projective direction lies outside both windows.

Every boundary edge occurrence of a bounded exceptional contact face is a terminal
occurrence of its complete stopped train.  Choose one off-window occurrence from each
of the `p_5` pentagons.  These are distinct face-edge incidences.  A complete stopped
train has exactly two terminal incidences in total, so one train can pay at most two of
the chosen pentagons. Consequently

\[
\boxed{2T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\ge p_5.}
\tag{GP.7}
\]

Combining (GP.6) and (GP.7),

\[
X+2T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\ge f.
\tag{GP.8}
\]

The proof-only AG42/Section-171 face bootstrap gives

\[
{f\over D}\ge f_*-o(1),
\tag{GP.9}
\]

which proves (GP.1).

## 3. Quantitative consequences

Equation (GP.1) has two useful forms.

First, if both normalized currencies were strictly below `f_*/3`, then

\[
X+2T_{off}<f_*D,
\]

contradicting (GP.1).  This proves (GP.3).

Second, on any subsequence with `X=o(D)`, equation (GP.1) gives the sharper

\[
T_{off}\ge(f_*/2-o(1))D,
\]

which is (GP.4).

This is much stronger than extracting off-window trains only from pentagons hit by
exceptional singleton trains.  It uses every pentagon forced by the global face floor.

## 4. Interaction with the current frontier

The theorem narrows two important branches.

1. **Low degree-excess branch.** If `Q-f=o(D)`, no pair of projective direction
   windows can carry all but `o(D)` stopped trains.  At least
   `0.0835623...D-o(D)` complete trains remain outside every such pair.

2. **Near-Mantel phase-star branch.** Existing near-Mantel reductions produce a small
   number of coherent phase windows.  Equation (GP.4) shows that, when degree excess is
   sublinear, a genuinely macroscopic third directional population is mandatory; a
   two-phase degeneration cannot absorb the exceptional-face population.

This does not by itself exclude a three-phase triangular-lattice-like survivor.  Such a
three-phase endpoint is compatible with near-Groemer pentagons and is precisely the
configuration that subsequent phase-star or source-minimality arguments must attack.

## 5. Scope audit

- `Q` and `f` are the canonical global bounded exceptional-face currencies.
- `T_off` counts complete stopped trains, not face incidences.  Division by two in
  (GP.7) is occurrence-safe because a complete train has exactly two terminal
  incidences.
- The odd-face theorem is applied only to actual pentagonal contact faces; no claim is
  made for even-degree faces.
- No singleton-train hypothesis, outer-detour bound, near-Mantel hypothesis, or
  force-hull residual hypothesis is used.
- The face floor inherits the proof-only AG42/Section-171 lifecycle status.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict sub-`D` source is produced here.

C-II and Level I remain open.
